WEBVTT

NOTE
Brasserie Lund quarterly review. Teams recording, 2026-07-16 10:00 (Europe/Stockholm).
Lena Strand, Marcus Ek, PO.

1
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.125
<v Marcus Ek>Lena! There you are.</v>

2
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:31.450
<v Lena Strand>Hi. Sorry, the link in the invite sent me to last quarter's meeting first.</v>

3
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:35.750
<v Marcus Ek>Teams does that to me every week.</v>

4
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:37.900
<v PO>Morning, Lena.</v>

5
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:42.650
<v Lena Strand>Morning. Is it just the three of us?</v>

6
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:48.675
<v Marcus Ek>Just us. Ulrika sends her apologies, she's with another chain this morning.</v>

7
00:00:49.575 --> 00:00:51.275
<v Lena Strand>Good for her.</v>

8
00:00:51.525 --> 00:00:59.675
<v Lena Strand>Half my kitchens are on summer temps this month, so nobody in my team goes anywhere in July.</v>

9
00:01:00.575 --> 00:01:05.700
<v Marcus Ek>How was the terrace season? You had that heat in June.</v>

10
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.150
<v Lena Strand>Good, actually. The Malmö terraces were full every night for three weeks.</v>

11
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:17.100
<v Lena Strand>Then it rained for a week and everyone stayed home.</v>

12
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.700
<v Marcus Ek>[laughs] Swedish summer.</v>

13
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.000
<v Lena Strand>Swedish summer. Are you in the office?</v>

14
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:29.175
<v Marcus Ek>I'm in the office. It's me and the plants.</v>

15
00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:36.075
<v PO>I'm at home. The building's got the air conditioning off for some reason.</v>

16
00:01:36.975 --> 00:01:39.525
<v Marcus Ek>They're saving it for August.</v>

17
00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:41.525
<v Lena Strand>[laughs]</v>

18
00:01:42.425 --> 00:01:47.975
<v PO>Should we get going? I know you've got a tight morning, Lena.</v>

19
00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:53.150
<v Lena Strand>Yes, please. I've got the regional managers at eleven.</v>

20
00:01:54.050 --> 00:02:02.200
<v Marcus Ek>So, quarterly review. I had a few slides, but honestly, Lena, you go first. What's on your list?</v>

21
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:07.375
<v Lena Strand>Good, because there's only one thing on my list.</v>

22
00:02:07.625 --> 00:02:08.725
<v Lena Strand>Christmas.</v>

23
00:02:09.625 --> 00:02:10.875
<v Marcus Ek>In July.</v>

24
00:02:11.775 --> 00:02:15.175
<v Lena Strand>In July. That's when we plan it.</v>

25
00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:22.925
<v Lena Strand>Christmas bookings open on the first of November. All forty restaurants, same day.</v>

26
00:02:23.175 --> 00:02:31.325
<v Lena Strand>And the good dates go in the first week. The Fridays in December are gone by the tenth.</v>

27
00:02:32.225 --> 00:02:33.475
<v Marcus Ek>That fast?</v>

28
00:02:34.375 --> 00:02:38.225
<v Lena Strand>That fast. Companies book their Christmas dinners early.</v>

29
00:02:38.475 --> 00:02:44.475
<v Lena Strand>Which is the problem, because they book early and then they don't come.</v>

30
00:02:44.725 --> 00:02:52.000
<v Lena Strand>December is where the no-shows hurt us most. I pulled the numbers before this call.</v>

31
00:02:52.250 --> 00:03:01.250
<v Lena Strand>Last December we lost, sorry, not lost, we had about one table a night per restaurant that didn't turn up.</v>

32
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:06.200
<v Lena Strand>One table a night. Forty restaurants. Every night in December.</v>

33
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.650
<v Marcus Ek>That's a lot of dinners.</v>

34
00:03:10.550 --> 00:03:17.400
<v Lena Strand>It's a lot of dinners. And the ones that don't come are the big ones.</v>

35
00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:24.925
<v Lena Strand>A table of twelve books in October, the company changes its plans, and nobody rings us.</v>

36
00:03:25.175 --> 00:03:33.325
<v Lena Strand>The table sits empty on a Friday in December, and we turned three other groups away for it.</v>

37
00:03:34.225 --> 00:03:38.075
<v PO>Did you try ringing them the day before?</v>

38
00:03:38.975 --> 00:03:45.825
<v Lena Strand>We did, in some restaurants. Managers calling every big booking by hand the day before.</v>

39
00:03:46.075 --> 00:03:53.775
<v Lena Strand>It helped a bit. It also cost me a manager's whole afternoon every day for a month.</v>

40
00:03:55.025 --> 00:03:59.725
<v Lena Strand>So, my ask. It's the same ask as in June.</v>

41
00:03:59.975 --> 00:04:05.525
<v Lena Strand>I need the no-show fees live before the Christmas bookings open.</v>

42
00:04:05.775 --> 00:04:09.625
<v Lena Strand>Not during December. Before the first of November.</v>

43
00:04:09.875 --> 00:04:18.875
<v Lena Strand>The fee has to be on the booking from the day the guest books it. Otherwise it's useless to me.</v>

44
00:04:19.775 --> 00:04:26.625
<v PO>Yes. If the card isn't taken when they book, we can't ask for it afterwards.</v>

45
00:04:27.525 --> 00:04:28.625
<v Lena Strand>Exactly.</v>

46
00:04:28.875 --> 00:04:35.725
<v Lena Strand>And in June, I wrote it down, you told me "Q4, before the Christmas season".</v>

47
00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:43.675
<v Lena Strand>I took that home and my managers asked me what Q4 means, and I couldn't tell them.</v>

48
00:04:43.925 --> 00:04:47.325
<v Lena Strand>Q4 could be the fifteenth of December.</v>

49
00:04:47.575 --> 00:04:51.850
<v Lena Strand>The fifteenth of December is no good to me.</v>

50
00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:57.225
<v Lena Strand>So I need something firmer than that. A month, at least.</v>

51
00:04:58.125 --> 00:05:01.100
<v Marcus Ek>That's fair. That's completely fair, Lena.</v>

52
00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:09.500
<v Marcus Ek>And I think we're in a better place to answer that than we were in June, aren't we?</v>

53
00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:20.175
<v PO>[pause] Let me be careful, because I know how a date travels once I've said it.</v>

54
00:05:21.075 --> 00:05:26.200
<v Marcus Ek>[laughs] It travels straight into my slides, is where it travels.</v>

55
00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.650
<v PO>That's what I'm afraid of.</v>

56
00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:35.450
<v PO>So. The part of this we didn't control was the card bit.</v>

57
00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:44.275
<v PO>Taking a card at booking and holding it to charge later. That sits with our payments team, not mine.</v>

58
00:05:44.525 --> 00:05:48.800
<v PO>That landed at the end of June. It's done.</v>

59
00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:54.600
<v PO>The rest is ours, and we start on it properly next week.</v>

60
00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:58.050
<v Lena Strand>Which means what, in months?</v>

61
00:06:00.450 --> 00:06:07.725
<v PO>We're aiming for the end of October, so you have it before the first of November.</v>

62
00:06:08.625 --> 00:06:10.325
<v Lena Strand>End of October.</v>

63
00:06:11.225 --> 00:06:15.075
<v PO>End of October. That's what we're building towards.</v>

64
00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:18.950
<v Marcus Ek>I'll put that in the renewal.</v>

65
00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:22.400
<v Lena Strand>[laughs] Good. Then it's real.</v>

66
00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:30.150
<v PO>Let me see the wording before it goes in, Marcus. But yes. That's the date.</v>

67
00:06:31.050 --> 00:06:37.050
<v Marcus Ek>You'll see the wording. I'm not going to write anything you haven't said.</v>

68
00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.850
<v Marcus Ek>You just said it, so.</v>

69
00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:43.300
<v PO>I did just say it.</v>

70
00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:50.200
<v Lena Strand>End of October works. If it's the twenty-ninth, it's the twenty-ninth.</v>

71
00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:56.000
<v Lena Strand>As long as it's switched on before the first, I don't mind.</v>

72
00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:58.600
<v PO>That's the plan.</v>

73
00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:08.075
<v Lena Strand>And that's for all forty? Not a trial in five restaurants and then the rest in the new year?</v>

74
00:07:08.975 --> 00:07:15.825
<v PO>All forty. It's a setting per restaurant, you turn it on where you want it.</v>

75
00:07:16.725 --> 00:07:18.425
<v Lena Strand>All forty, then.</v>

76
00:07:20.175 --> 00:07:27.450
<v Lena Strand>Can I ask how it actually works? Because that's the next thing my managers will ask.</v>

77
00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:29.600
<v PO>Of course.</v>

78
00:07:29.850 --> 00:07:37.550
<v PO>The guest books the way they do today. There's one more step, they put a card in.</v>

79
00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.075
<v PO>Nothing is charged at that point. It's just held.</v>

80
00:07:42.975 --> 00:07:44.675
<v Lena Strand>Held how long?</v>

81
00:07:45.575 --> 00:07:52.000
<v PO>Until the booking. If they turn up, nothing happens, the card is never charged.</v>

82
00:07:52.250 --> 00:08:01.675
<v PO>If they don't turn up, the restaurant marks the booking as a no-show that evening, the way you do now.</v>

83
00:08:01.925 --> 00:08:06.625
<v PO>And the next morning the fee goes on the card.</v>

84
00:08:06.875 --> 00:08:14.575
<v PO>The guest gets a receipt by text, so there's no surprise on their statement a week later.</v>

85
00:08:15.475 --> 00:08:18.875
<v Lena Strand>The morning after. Not the same night?</v>

86
00:08:19.775 --> 00:08:21.475
<v PO>The morning after.</v>

87
00:08:21.725 --> 00:08:32.025
<v PO>It gives the restaurant the night to fix a mistake. If someone marked the wrong table, they can undo it before anything's charged.</v>

88
00:08:32.925 --> 00:08:38.050
<v Lena Strand>Good, because that will happen. Someone will mark the wrong table.</v>

89
00:08:38.950 --> 00:08:40.200
<v Marcus Ek>Every Friday.</v>

90
00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:44.500
<v Lena Strand>Every Friday, at ten, when it's loud.</v>

91
00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:48.150
<v Lena Strand>And bookings we take over the phone?</v>

92
00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:57.625
<v PO>No card on those, so no fee. It's for bookings that come in through the website, or through Google.</v>

93
00:08:58.525 --> 00:09:03.225
<v Lena Strand>Fine. Phone is a small share for us now anyway.</v>

94
00:09:03.475 --> 00:09:08.175
<v Lena Strand>What does the guest see? When they book, I mean.</v>

95
00:09:09.075 --> 00:09:17.225
<v PO>A line under the booking that says there's a fee if they don't turn up, and the amount.</v>

96
00:09:17.475 --> 00:09:21.750
<v PO>Then the card. Then the confirmation, same as today.</v>

97
00:09:22.650 --> 00:09:26.050
<v Lena Strand>And the amount is ours to set.</v>

98
00:09:26.950 --> 00:09:29.500
<v PO>Yours to set. Per restaurant.</v>

99
00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.100
<v Lena Strand>Per restaurant. Okay.</v>

100
00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:39.350
<v Lena Strand>So here's my question, and I think I asked this in June too.</v>

101
00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.875
<v Lena Strand>Can the fee be different for lunch and dinner?</v>

102
00:09:44.125 --> 00:09:51.825
<v Lena Strand>A lunch table is maybe a hundred kronor of lost business. A Christmas dinner table is thousands.</v>

103
00:09:52.075 --> 00:09:58.925
<v Lena Strand>Charging the same for both feels wrong, and the lunch guests will complain about it.</v>

104
00:09:59.825 --> 00:10:03.225
<v PO>You did ask in June. I remember.</v>

105
00:10:03.475 --> 00:10:07.750
<v PO>The honest answer is not in the first release.</v>

106
00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:15.275
<v PO>It's one fee per restaurant to start with. You set an amount, and that's the amount.</v>

107
00:10:16.175 --> 00:10:17.875
<v Lena Strand>For every booking.</v>

108
00:10:18.775 --> 00:10:23.050
<v PO>For every booking with a card on it, yes.</v>

109
00:10:23.300 --> 00:10:29.300
<v PO>We parked the lunch and dinner question in June and it's still parked.</v>

110
00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:37.250
<v PO>I would rather give you one fee at the end of October than two fees in December.</v>

111
00:10:40.150 --> 00:10:43.125
<v Lena Strand>[pause] No, I agree with that.</v>

112
00:10:43.375 --> 00:10:52.375
<v Lena Strand>I can live with one fee for Christmas. I'll set it for the dinners and lunch is what it is.</v>

113
00:10:52.625 --> 00:10:56.900
<v Lena Strand>But I want it on the list for after.</v>

114
00:10:57.800 --> 00:10:59.925
<v PO>It's on the list.</v>

115
00:11:00.825 --> 00:11:10.250
<v Marcus Ek>And that's exactly the sort of thing we can look at again in the spring, once it's been running a while.</v>

116
00:11:11.150 --> 00:11:14.550
<v PO>Once it's been running, yes. Not before.</v>

117
00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:17.150
<v Marcus Ek>Not before. Obviously.</v>

118
00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:19.750
<v Lena Strand>[laughs] You two.</v>

119
00:11:21.500 --> 00:11:24.475
<v Lena Strand>Right. That's really my whole list.</v>

120
00:11:25.375 --> 00:11:31.800
<v Marcus Ek>Then let me do one quick thing from my side, because you'll like it.</v>

121
00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:38.900
<v Marcus Ek>The areas thing went out this week. Window, terrace, bar. Guests pick when they book.</v>

122
00:11:39.800 --> 00:11:46.225
<v Lena Strand>I saw that. One of the Malmö managers had it switched on by Tuesday.</v>

123
00:11:47.125 --> 00:11:48.225
<v Marcus Ek>Already?</v>

124
00:11:49.125 --> 00:11:53.825
<v Lena Strand>They don't wait for me. It's fine, it looks good.</v>

125
00:11:54.725 --> 00:11:59.850
<v PO>Good to hear. You're the first customer I've heard that from.</v>

126
00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:06.025
<v Lena Strand>Oh, one small thing, while I have you both.</v>

127
00:12:06.275 --> 00:12:13.550
<v Lena Strand>At two of our restaurants, Malmö and Lund, guests have been getting the reminder text twice.</v>

128
00:12:13.800 --> 00:12:17.200
<v Lena Strand>Same text, twice, a few minutes apart.</v>

129
00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:24.725
<v Lena Strand>Not a big thing, but people ring us to ask if something's wrong with the booking.</v>

130
00:12:24.975 --> 00:12:33.975
<v Lena Strand>And then a manager has to look it up and say no, you're fine, it's one table, we've got you.</v>

131
00:12:34.875 --> 00:12:37.425
<v Marcus Ek>Twice? The exact same text?</v>

132
00:12:38.325 --> 00:12:45.600
<v Lena Strand>As far as I know. I haven't seen one myself, it's what the managers tell me.</v>

133
00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:49.900
<v PO>How long has that been going on?</v>

134
00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:59.800
<v Lena Strand>A few weeks? It came up at the managers' meeting last week, so at least since the end of June.</v>

135
00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:06.900
<v Lena Strand>It might be longer. Nobody would have mentioned it if the guests hadn't started ringing.</v>

136
00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:10.350
<v PO>Okay. We'll look into it.</v>

137
00:13:10.600 --> 00:13:16.150
<v PO>If you can send me exactly which two restaurants, that would help.</v>

138
00:13:17.050 --> 00:13:25.625
<v Lena Strand>I'll send you the names. We've got three in Lund, so I need to check which one it is.</v>

139
00:13:26.525 --> 00:13:29.925
<v PO>Thanks. I'll find out what's going on.</v>

140
00:13:30.825 --> 00:13:37.675
<v Lena Strand>Like I said, it's not a big thing. Nobody has lost a booking over it.</v>

141
00:13:37.925 --> 00:13:44.350
<v Lena Strand>It's just the phone calls. December is bad enough for phone calls without that.</v>

142
00:13:45.250 --> 00:13:47.375
<v Marcus Ek>We'll get it sorted.</v>

143
00:13:49.125 --> 00:13:55.125
<v Marcus Ek>So, let me make sure I've got this before Lena has to run.</v>

144
00:13:55.375 --> 00:13:59.650
<v Marcus Ek>Lena, you want the October date in writing. Yes?</v>

145
00:14:00.550 --> 00:14:09.550
<v Lena Strand>I'd like that. Something I can put in front of the regional managers, so it isn't just me saying it.</v>

146
00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:15.575
<v PO>I'll send you the October plan in writing by next Friday.</v>

147
00:14:15.825 --> 00:14:22.675
<v PO>What's in the first release, what isn't, and the date. One page, not a deck.</v>

148
00:14:23.575 --> 00:14:28.275
<v Lena Strand>One page is perfect. Next Friday is the twenty-fourth?</v>

149
00:14:29.175 --> 00:14:31.300
<v PO>The twenty-fourth, yes.</v>

150
00:14:32.200 --> 00:14:36.475
<v Marcus Ek>And I'll send the renewal paperwork over by Wednesday.</v>

151
00:14:36.725 --> 00:14:44.000
<v Marcus Ek>With the October line in it, and the price we talked about in June, nothing new.</v>

152
00:14:44.900 --> 00:14:53.050
<v Lena Strand>Wednesday. Good. I'll get it to our legal the same day so it doesn't sit on my desk.</v>

153
00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:58.225
<v Marcus Ek>You're the best customer we've got, you know that.</v>

154
00:14:59.125 --> 00:15:02.975
<v Lena Strand>[laughs] You say that to all of them.</v>

155
00:15:03.875 --> 00:15:07.275
<v Marcus Ek>Only to you. And maybe two others.</v>

156
00:15:08.175 --> 00:15:16.750
<v Lena Strand>And I'll send the names of the two restaurants. Today, if I can catch the Lund manager before service.</v>

157
00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:20.625
<v PO>Thank you. That'd be a help.</v>

158
00:15:21.525 --> 00:15:26.225
<v Lena Strand>Was there anything else from your side? You said slides.</v>

159
00:15:27.125 --> 00:15:31.825
<v Marcus Ek>They can wait. It was mostly numbers you already know.</v>

160
00:15:32.075 --> 00:15:37.200
<v Marcus Ek>Actually, there was one thing, the, no. Forget it. Next time.</v>

161
00:15:38.100 --> 00:15:39.350
<v Lena Strand>Next time.</v>

162
00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:44.725
<v Lena Strand>Right, I have to run. Thank you both, this was useful.</v>

163
00:15:45.625 --> 00:15:49.475
<v PO>Thanks, Lena. You'll have the page next Friday.</v>

164
00:15:50.375 --> 00:15:53.350
<v Marcus Ek>Thank you, Lena. Enjoy the rain.</v>

165
00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:55.500
<v Lena Strand>[laughs] Bye.</v>

166
00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:57.500
<v PO>Bye.</v>

167
00:15:58.400 --> 00:15:59.650
<v Marcus Ek>Bye, bye.</v>
